---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

// The "Delete data URL" entered in Play Console's Data safety form for our
// Google Play apps. Play wants a page a user can reach to delete, or request
// deletion of, the data the app collected. Ours is held by Google Play Games
// Services (leaderboards), so this page walks the user through Google's own
// per-game deletion and offers email as the request fallback. Companion to
// /privacy/apps, which is the full policy. The deletion steps follow Google's
// "Delete your Play Games data or Play Games profile" help article
// (support.google.com/googleplay/answer/9130646, checked September 24, 2026).
// Since Delve, Die, Repeat 2.1, a bug report the player chooses to send
// (☰ REPORT A BUG) is the one thing we hold ourselves: it sits in the game's
// Firebase project and is posted publicly on GitHub, so deletion is by
// request to us.
const contact = "sheibeck@gmail.com";
const subject = encodeURIComponent("Delete my Delve, Die, Repeat leaderboard data");
const bugSubject = encodeURIComponent("Delete my Delve, Die, Repeat bug report");
---
<Layout
  title="Delete your data — Google Play apps"
  description="How to delete the leaderboard data collected by Darktier Studios apps on Google Play, or a bug report you sent, or request that we help you delete it."
>
  <div class="container">
    <section style="padding:84px 0 28px">
      <p class="kicker">Darktier Studios · legal · Google Play apps</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,72px);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0 0 0 -0.06em">Delete your data</h1>
      <p class="privacy-meta">Applies to <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> (<code>com.darktierstudios.delvedierepeat</code>) · {site.legalName}</p>
    </section>

    <article class="privacy">
      <p class="privacy-lede">
        Our apps send data anywhere in only two cases. Your <strong>leaderboard data</strong> is sent only
        while the in-game <strong>Compete</strong> setting is on: your Google Play Games player ID, the
        scores for each finished run, and the short run summary listed in our
        <a href="/privacy/apps">privacy policy</a>. It is held by Google, not by us, and you can delete it
        yourself in about a minute. A <strong>bug report</strong> is sent only if you write one and tap
        <strong>Send</strong>; we delete it on request, as described <a href="#bug-reports">below</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Delete your leaderboard data</h2>
      <p>On the web:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Go to <a href="https://play.google.com/games/profile" target="_blank" rel="noopener">play.google.com/games/profile</a>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Your Data</strong>, click <strong>Delete Play Games Account &amp; Data</strong>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Delete individual game data</strong>, find <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> and click <strong>Delete</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Or in the Google Play Store app on your Android device:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Tap your profile icon at the top right, then <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
        <li>Tap <strong>General</strong>, then <strong>Play Games Profile</strong>, then <strong>Privacy &amp; Settings</strong>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Your Data</strong>, tap <strong>Delete profile &amp; game data</strong>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Delete individual game data</strong>, find <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> and tap <strong>Delete</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That removes your Play Games data for the game, including your scores on every leaderboard you
        appeared on. Google describes the same steps in its
        <a href="https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/9130646" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Games data help</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Stop sending new data</h2>
      <p>
        In the game, open the account menu (the small face at the top right of the title screen, or beside
        the ☰ menu in the dungeon) and choose <strong>STOP COMPETING</strong>, or set
        <strong>COMPETE</strong> to <strong>OFF</strong>. With Compete off, the game sends nothing to Google
        Play Games, discards any runs still waiting to be sent, and stays fully playable. To disconnect the
        game from Play Games entirely, use the Play Games app.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bug-reports">Delete a bug report</h2>
      <p>
        A report sent from <strong>REPORT A BUG</strong> in the game's ☰ menu carries no account, Google
        Play Games identity, or contact detail. It is stored in the game's Google Firebase project and posted
        publicly as an issue on the game's GitHub repository
        (<a href="https://github.com/sheibeck/ddr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/sheibeck/ddr</a>).
        To have one deleted, email
        <a href={`mailto:${contact}?subject=${bugSubject}`}>{contact}</a> with the link to its GitHub issue,
        or roughly when you sent it and what it said. We will delete it from Firebase and GitHub and confirm
        when it's done.
      </p>

      <h2>Everything else is already yours</h2>
      <p>
        Your current run, graveyard, personal bests, and settings live only on your device. Uninstalling the
        app deletes them. Apart from a bug report you chose to send, we hold no copy of anything — we run no
        servers of our own and collect nothing automatically — so there is nothing further for us to erase.
      </p>

      <h2>Need help?</h2>
      <p>
        If you can't complete the steps above, email
        <a href={`mailto:${contact}?subject=${subject}`}>{contact}</a> from any address and tell us the
        Play Games profile name you used. Since we don't hold the data, we'll work with Google Play Games
        support on your behalf and confirm when it's done.
      </p>

      <p>
        Full policy: <a href="/privacy/apps">privacy policy for our apps</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
